===Etymology===
The origin of sense 1 (“a state of bliss”) is uncertain; however, the following etymology has been suggested:

* The first edition of the ''{{w|International Cloud Atlas}}'' (1896),<ref>{{cite-book|author=International Meteorological Committee|authorlink=International Meteorological Organization|author2=H[ugo Hildebrand] Hildebrandsson|authorlink2=Hugo Hildebrand Hildebrandsson|author3=A[lbert] Riggenbach|authorlink3=Albert Riggenbach|author4=L[éon] Teisserenc de Bort|authorlink4=Léon Teisserenc de Bort|title=[[w:International Cloud Atlas|Atlas international des nuages = International Cloud-atlas = Internationaler Wolken-Atlas]]|location=Paris|publisher=Gauthier-Villars|year=1896|oclc=715068869}}.</ref> which defined ten types of cloud, described the ninth type as the [[cumulonimbus]] which rises to 10 km (6.2 miles), the highest a cloud can be.<ref name="Etymonline">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|entry=cloud nine|accessdate=22 December 2017}}</ref>

Compare {{m|en|cloud seven||state of complete happiness or euphoria}},<ref>{{cite-book|author=Harold Wentworth and {{w|Stuart Berg Flexner}}, compilers and editors|title=Dictionary of American Slang|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Thomas T. Crowell|year=1960|oclc=803362919}}.</ref> which may have originated from confusion of ''cloud nine'' with {{m|en|seventh heaven}}.<ref name="Etymonline"/>

Sense 2 (“a state of fantastic or impractical dreaming or thinking”) may be due to a confusion between sense 1 and the phrase {{m|en|head in the clouds}}.

